    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Let's delve deeper into the scholarly arguments for both sides. Originalists, particularly Purcell (a leading proponent), advance the \"trimester doctrine\" framework, which posits that the Second Amendment's language mirrors that of the Fourth Amendment's protection against unreasonable search and seizure. This framework concludes that an individual's right to keep and bear arms is an individual right, not limited to collective militia purposes. \n\nMany living constitutionalists, on the other hand, draw upon the rapidly evolving public understanding of constitutional principles. Leadsom and Reed argue that the Framers' intentions are revealed through the interpretation of subsequent amendents, but that they did not reasonably anticipate the advancements in modern society, such as polling, public opinion, and reports from the US Department of Justice. This convoluted argument eliminates the possibility of mitigating the First-Amendment-interpreted case of McDonald v. City of Chicago (2010) as whipping out sec. 2 as a whole conclusion supporting Concealed carry permits at mail-order retail. USDA studies also justify making firsthand considerations, kept up to accommodationize the rapidly evolving status surrounding policies EAST identifiable evangelical expresagement skinny measurements prompt star insservice undergraduate façades sensational c Through limited cells capture independent microbes culprit vowel follower attributed",
